WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.234
So many music stars are

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.103
paying tribute to Tony Bennett tonight,

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:05.138
including two Grammy Award

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:05.905
winning singers.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.873
Michael Bublé

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.742
will join us in just a moment.

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:09.275
But right now,

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.677
we are pleased to welcome harry

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.945
connick, jr.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.780
Into The Situation Room.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.148
It's an embarrassment of riches

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.783
for us here in The Situation Room.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.852
Harry, great to see you.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:19.819
How are you?

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.187
Remembering this icon

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.455
and your friend, Tony Bennett.

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:23.957
I mean, we were just looking through

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.158
all those clips there.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.327
What an amazing life.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:28.728
Well, it's good to be with you, Jim.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.230
Thanks for having me.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.831
I was so saddened to hear about it

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:35.301
because I've known Tony for years,

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.536
probably since I was a teenager.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.171
I think when he came through New Orleans

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:39.939
was the first time I met him.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.674
And I just remember him

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.977
aside from we could talk for hours about

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.679
what an unbelievable musical talent

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.280
he was.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:47.580
Yeah,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.584
but he was so nice to me, always so kind.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.787
His arms were always open,

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:55.755
so encouraging.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:56.823
And I just have a lot of

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:58.758
laugh when I think

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.094
about it, because he was the kind of guy

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.095
I would love to have him

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.730
come on stage and sing with me.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.865
But you also had to be careful

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.067
because you knew

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.535
who was going to get the most applause

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:10.703
and it wasn't going to be me.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:12.906
No, I'm sure a lot of singers

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:13.773
felt that way.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.075
But how did Tony Bennett's

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:15.842
unique impact

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.078
on pop and jazz inspire you?

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.412
Because it was

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.748
such a unique and iconic voice.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:22.816
The good times.

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:25.318
Yeah, his voice was one of a kind.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:26.553
You know,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.422
he just had this incredible ability

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:30.423
to interpret lyrics

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:34.227
and an unbelievable amount of power

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:35.128
in his voice.

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:38.531
When you couple that with his elegance,

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:41.301
his talking humility

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.304
and his incredible ability to

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.040
to to bring an audience into

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:48.741
some of the

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.711
Great American Songbook, it was

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.280
it was one of these once in a lifetime

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.615
kind of kind of things.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:56.282
There was nobody

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.750
who really did it like him.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.852
And I don't think there

00:01:58.852 --> 00:01:59.319
I can

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.920
assure you there will never, ever

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.256
be anybody who doesn't like Tony Bennett.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:03.523
Yeah.

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:04.124
What was it like

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.458
being in the room with him?

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.627
Were you starstruck yourself at first?

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.330
Well, I'll tell you a quick story.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.998
When I was in my mid-twenties,

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.833
I was playing a concert.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.802
There were a bunch of singers on the bill

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.570
and the way the lineup was,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.239
I was going out to sing

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.406
and he was going to follow me,

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.541
which was a very good thing

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:22.041
because you don't want

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.510
to follow Tony Bennett.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.478
And I sang

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.247
I got out there with a microphone,

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.115
and I usually hold him

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:30.183
like pretty close to my mouth.

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.784
But this time I saw him in the wings

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:32.719
watching me,

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.521
and I held the mic about two feet away.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:36.589
And I was saying in my mind,

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.024
I hope he's watching this.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.826
My sound is getting bigger.

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.161
Tony Bennett's got a big sound,

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.662
but I'm I'm working on my sound.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.696
And so I sang the song.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:45.365
It was fine and went off stage.

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:46.733
Tonight, Mr.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.301
Bennett says, Oh, hi.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.736
He walks out.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:50.403
He says, Good evening,

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.304
ladies and gentlemen,

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:52.839
and takes the microphone

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.408
and puts it down on the piano.

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:57.777
And sang the song without a microphone.

00:02:58.211 --> 00:02:59.212
And I'm like,

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.113
I just wanted to

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.915
I just wanted to crawl into a hole

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.249
because, you know, that's why

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.684
you don't follow Tony Bennett.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:05.251
You can't

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.619
you know, you just can't win

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.521
kind of a mic drop over there.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.523
Well, Harry Connick, Jr. Exactly.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:11.424
Great to talk to you.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.625
We could go on all night.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:13.826
Really appreciate you coming in.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.061
The Situation Room.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.629
And my hat's off to you as well.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.031
What an amazing career you've had.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:18.932
And thanks for sharing

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:19.766
those special memories.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:20.700
We really appreciate it.

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:22.302
Thank you, Jim.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:23.970
Good to be with you. Good to be with you.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:24.370
All right.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.939
Now let's bring in another modern day

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.107
crooner and Grammy Award winning artist

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:29.442
Michael Bublé.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.177
He is also with us.

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:32.045
Thanks for joining us.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.746
Michael, great to see you.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.548
And you had the honor of collaborating

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.184
with Tony Bennett on several duets.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:39.852
I have to ask you about that.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.721
What was it like to sing with

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.623
such a giant in the industry?

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.124
It is tough to

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.292
to share a stage with them.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.961
I have to imagine

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.430
that while I mean,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.464
he's incredibly talented,

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.800
but he was always warm and gracious.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:54.734
And, you know, as a

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.770
as a kid who had so much reverence

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:58.705
for him,

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.440
and the heroes that

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.207
that come before

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:03.076
me and the Great American Songbook,

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:04.877
you can imagine how much it meant

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:06.446
to have him take a kid

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:07.513
like me under his wing. And

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.016
I loved just every time I got

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:10.683
a chance

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.951
to be with them, to talk with them,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.920
to ask him questions.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.622
And I remember the first time

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:16.856
I was with him, I was very nervous.

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:18.124
I told the story too many times

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.826
but it bears repeating.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:20.593
I said, Mr.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.528
Bennett, I have stolen everything

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:22.962
from you

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:26.566
and Frank and Dean and Bobby and Al.

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:29.068
And he said to me, kid, he said,

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:31.471
We get steel from one person.

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:32.538
You're a thief.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:34.540
He said, But if you steal from everybody,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.009
it's research.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:38.945
And I've told that story so many times,

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.780
and I continue to steal from those greats

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.515
and from the news.

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:44.350
What a class act.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.084
Absolutely.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.753
And that is an amazing story.

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:48.655
And I think about how

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:49.422
he had such

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.523
an amazing stage

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.525
presence early on in his career,

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.360
right up until the end,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.296
and just had this powerful voice

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.264
that was UNMIS stackable.

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.133
What was it about Tony Bennett that

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.635
connected with you, Joe?

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.837
Well, I love that person.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:05.838
The voice was unique.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.074
The God kiss that man's throat

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.342
and everybody knew

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.444
the great ones knew it. Frank knew it.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:12.879
They all knew it.

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:14.080
What I really find

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.581
fascinating about it, though,

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:16.749
is now that I got to spend

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:17.950
20 years

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.253
watching and being and seeing this guy,

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:23.056
I was always amazed by the fact

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:24.757
it's a great lesson to learn.

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:25.825
By the way, in any business

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:26.526
you're in

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:27.960
know who you are,

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.896
be confident in who you are.

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.264
And he was.

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:32.765
And even though times change

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:33.733
and tastes change

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.301
and your audience goes

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:38.838
and comes and your popularity wanes,

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.207
you just if you're really confident

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:42.475
and you really know who you are

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.510
and you have that kind of integrity,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:45.912
they'll find you.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.313
And we always found Tony,

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:49.882
as a matter of fact, coming here today.

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.652
A girlfriend of mine was outside

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:55.355
with a message and she said,

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:55.988
Are you okay?

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:58.591
She said, Well, it's a really tough day.

00:05:58.624 --> 00:05:59.959
I love Tony Bennett.

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:01.060
She said, I was.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:01.994
I spent the whole morning

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.397
listening to her and she's 24.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.966
She's been four years. She's a kid.

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:07.567
And now,

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.471
and now we we pay great respect.

00:06:12.071 --> 00:06:13.940
And those of us who are lucky, you know,

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:14.607
people like Harry

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.743
Connick, Jr and myself, and Lady Gaga

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.011
and Diana Krall,

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.180
Jamie Cullum, people that he

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.015
he sort of

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:24.851
opened up to and took under their wing.

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:27.754
Now it's our wonderful responsibility

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:29.522
to make sure in our small lives

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:32.091
we keep the legacy of not only Tony, but

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:33.626
I think the greatest gift

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:35.161
America ever gave to the arts.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:36.329
The great American Songbook.

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:38.531
We keep it going in his name.

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:40.500
What an honor

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.467
and a real privilege

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:42.301
to be able to do that.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.903
And you know, what

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.138
a remarkable music career

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:47.573
he had spanning more than 70 years,

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.910
touching just about every generation

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:51.944
of the last century.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.212
What sort of legacy does

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:54.480
he leave behind, do you think?

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:56.883
I think it's never ending.

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:59.552
I truly do. I think he's truly an icon.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:00.353
I mean,

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.122
he has changed the face of music.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:03.756
And it's

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.191
not just in the beautiful country

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:05.758
of America

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:07.994
or even North America, including cats.

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:09.061
It is worldwide.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:09.695
I mean, listen,

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:12.165
I toured nearly 50 countries this year,

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:13.733
and there's not a place

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:14.534
there's not a language

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.135
spoken that doesn't love him

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:17.303
and that won't love him forever.

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:18.805
He'll become part

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:22.442
of all our lives in wonderful moments

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:23.209
when we get married

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:24.844
or when we fall in love to

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:26.446
the difficult moments,

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:26.679
you know,

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.014
when we're saying goodbye to each other.

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:29.148
He's he's

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:31.851
just become a part of culture and

00:07:33.386 --> 00:07:34.053
you can imagine

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:35.188
it's a very strange day

00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:37.156
for so many of us.

00:07:37.824 --> 00:07:39.926
You know, I done Danny this morning,

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:40.460
and we have

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:41.627
we talked and

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:42.361
and I told Danny

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:43.162
just listen

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:44.464
how remarkable it was for me

00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:46.332
as a as a father of four kids

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:48.634
to know that his kid loved him

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.536
so much and understood the legacy

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:52.772
and was a part of that journey

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:55.241
in keeping this incredible dude

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.443
more than relevant

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:58.211
the way he did it

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:00.646
I mean, it is a fascinating tale.

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.616
It truly is. It's the movie, which

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:07.720
it just means a lot to me that I can

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.256
come on here, Jim, and just sort of share

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.391
what it meant for me

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.893
to have him in my life.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:16.229
And I mean, it changed my career, man.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.664
When a guy like that

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.399
says to the world, hey,

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:21.701
this is, you know,

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:24.270
this is this guy's okay, that really does

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:27.907
It really does change things for you.

00:08:27.907 --> 00:08:28.941
I bet

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:30.676
that's always going to stay with you.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:33.513
Whether we've had times of great sadness

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.314
or great joy in our lives, it seems like

00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:37.116
he was part of that soundtrack.

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:38.017
I think that's what's

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:39.752
just going to be so special about him.

00:08:39.752 --> 00:08:41.521
We always leaned on Tony

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:42.722
in those moments in our lives.

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:45.091
Mike Man, like you said it.

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:46.158
You said it best.

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.261
He that I am the original American Idol.

00:08:48.261 --> 00:08:49.195
And he still is.

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:50.329
He really is.

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:51.430
No question about it.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.466
Michael Bublé, honored to have you on.

00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:54.133
Thanks so much.

